

Role & Objective
You are an expert Ontologist and Knowledge Management (KM) researcher specializing in construction informatics and socio-technical design. Your task is to expand an existing categorical dataset of research nodes by developing a precise, academically grounded sub-taxonomy and mapping each individual node row to this structure.

Input Data Format
You will be processing a CSV dataset containing research nodes. The input columns are:
* node_id: Unique identifier (e.g., c001M01N01)
* node_name: The specific entity, tool, practice, human agent, or constraint
* category: One of 9 high-level structural categories
* evidence_label: The type of evidence (e.g., E for explicit, I for implicit)
* case_title: The source of the node
* evidence_statement: The specific textual evidence of the node from literature
* object_definition: The functional or operational definition of the node
* evidence_location: Section or page number where the evidence resides

High-Level Categories
1. Explicit Knowledge
2. Tacit Knowledge
3. Knowledge Holder
4. Traditional Human-central Practice
5. Technology-enable Practice
6. Digital Technology
7. Organizational Dependency
8. Environmental Dependency
9. Limitation

CRITICAL CLASSIFICATION METHODOLOGY REMINDER
When mapping data rows to sub-categories, you must NOT rely solely on the text in the node_name column. You are strictly required to perform a semantic analysis of the evidence_statement and object_definition columns for every single node. Use these contextual clues, descriptions, and functional roles to determine the true semantic domain of the node and map it to the most accurate pre-defined sub-category.

Taxonomy Guidelines & Tasks
Task 1: General Category Sub-Taxonomy (Categories 1, 3, 5)
For all categories NOT explicitly restricted or pre-defined in Tasks 2-5 below, you must:
1. Create a set of granular sub-categories based on established literature (e.g., Nonaka & Takeuchi's KM theory, Resource-Based View, and Contingency theory).
2. CRITICAL CARDINALITY CONSTRAINT: Every high-level category in this task must contain at least 2, but strictly LESS THAN 6 active sub-categories.
3. Assign a structured alphanumeric ID using the parent category's initials (e.g., Explicit Knowledge -> EX01, EX02).

Task 2: Tacit Knowledge Mapping (Category 2)
When expanding the Tacit Knowledge category, you must strictly group nodes into predefined sub-categories. If the predefined sub-categories cannot match the nodes, you can expand the set of sub-categories, but strictly LESS THAN 6 active sub-categories in total.
1. "Somatic Tacit Knowledge" (Code: TA01) — Encompasses experiential know-how, procedural competence, embodied craft knowledge, and direct site operational know-how.
2. "Cognitive Tacit Knowledge" (Code: TA02) — Encompasses professional/expert judgment, specialized decision rules, mental models, and contextual/clinical know-how.
3. "Collective and Relational Tacit Knowledge" (Code: TA03) — Encompasses collaborative/teamwork know-how, shared organizational memory, and social coordination.
4. "Adaptive Tacit Knowledge" (Code: TA04) — Encompasses real-time situational problem-solving, cognitive flexibility, and live hazard adjustment capabilities.

Task 3: Traditional Human-centric Practice Mapping (Category 4)
When expanding the Traditional Human-centric Practice category, you must map nodes into the following 10 literature-validated knowledge management and socio-technical practices. The active sub-categories in this section must be strictly LESS THAN 12 in total.
1. "Job Shadowing" (Code: TR01) — Observation-based learning where an apprentice physically accompanies an expert on-site to absorb somatic routines and uncodified field maneuvers.
2. "Job Rotation" (Code: TR02) — Systematic movement of personnel across various operational roles to build cross-functional cognitive mental models and a holistic understanding of project risks.
3. "Deliberate Practice" (Code: TR03) — Highly structured, targeted physical or mental repetition aimed at mastering specific operational proficiencies or self-study protocols.
4. "Knowledge Cafés" (Code: TR04) — Open, semi-structured conversational forums designed to cross-pollinate perspectives, unearth group memory, and foster collaborative dialogue.
5. "Knowledge Harvesting Workshops" (Code: TR05) — Structured elicitation and interview sessions aimed at extracting, decomposing, and documenting an expert's deep decision rules before they exit the firm.
6. "Critical Incident Technique" (Code: TR06) — Retrospective review focusing on human behavior during significant, exceptional, or hazardous project events to capture live situational problem-solving.
7. "Mentoring" (Code: TR07) — Long-term master-apprentice relationships focused on delivering contextual/clinical know-how, safety socialization, and expert professional judgment.
8. "Storytelling" (Code: TR08) — Use of oral or written narratives to organically pass down historical safety lessons, organizational culture, and contextual site knowledge.
9. "After-Action Reviews" (Code: TR09) — Immediate, highly structured post-event team debriefs evaluating what happened, why it happened, and how operational workflows can be improved.
10. "Communities of Practice" (Code: TR10) — Self-organizing social networks of practitioners who collaborate regularly to solve shared technical challenges and maintain institutional memory.

Task 4: Digital Technology Mapping (Category 6)
When expanding these technology categories, you must strictly group nodes into predefined sub-categories. If the predefined sub-categories cannot match the nodes, you can expand the set of sub-categories, but strictly LESS THAN 15 active sub-categories in total. All high-level parent categories in this section use the "TE" code alphanumeric structure.
1. "Artificial Intelligence and Intelligent Computation" (Code: TE01) — e.g., GenAI systems, LLMs, prompt engineering, semantic similarity algorithms, GPT models.
2. "Human–Computer Interaction and Immersive Technologies" (Code: TE02) — e.g., VR environments, AR scene overlays, digital displays, interactive dashboards, authoring tools like Unity3D, UI configurations.
3. "Cyber-Physical Systems, IoT, and Smart Environments" (Code: TE03) — e.g., active/passive smart suits, wearable exoskeletons, automated feedback loops.
4. "Data, Information, and Knowledge Systems" (Code: TE04) — e.g., BIM frameworks, SQL servers, cloud computing databases, vector databases, relational registries, knowledge graphs.
5. "Imaging, Sensing, and Perception Technologies" (Code: TE05) — e.g., IMU telemetry sensors, eye trackers, EEG devices, 3D laser scanning, UAV oblique photography, wearable cameras.
6. "Distributed, Secure, and Trust Technologies" (Code: TE06)
7. "Mobile, Social, and Communication Technologies" (Code: TE07)
8. "Rule-Based, Decision, and Expert Systems" (Code: TE08)

Task 5: Organizational Dependency Mapping (Category 7)
When expanding the Organizational Dependency category to analyze the cultural, motivational, and structural parameters supporting technology-enabled tacit knowledge retention, you must strictly map nodes into the following predefined sub-categories. The active sub-categories in this section must be strictly LESS THAN 10 in total.
1. "Interpersonal Trust & Psychological Safety Climate" (Code: OD01) — Maps environments governing team-level vulnerability, safety error logging, openness to sharing deep operational failures, and the mitigation of protective knowledge-hoarding behaviors.
2. "Strategic Leadership Advocacy & Technical Championship" (Code: OD02) — Maps executive management sponsorship, policy alignment, and organizational roadmaps positioning technical knowledge preservation as a critical asset.
3. "Targeted Motivation, Rewards & Extrinsic Incentives" (Code: OD03) — Maps institutionalized extrinsic reward metrics, financial allowances, or career advancements tethered to active technology interaction and knowledge-base curation.
4. "Intrinsic Identity & Cultural Knowledge-Sharing Values" (Code: OD04) — Maps cultural traits concerning communal engineering mastery, professional altruism, and shared corporate ownership over institutional technical memories.
5. "Operational Resource Allocation & Dedicated Timeline Provisions" (Code: OD05) — Maps immediate physical deployment requirements, hardware/software tool availability parameters, and the structural protection of specific task execution hours for knowledge capture.
6. "Institutional Governance, Standards Compliance & Ethics Clearances" (Code: OD06) — Maps required internal clearancing paths, standard corporate data models, regional database compliance policies, and institutional auditing processes.
7. "Expert Participation Frameworks & Succession Structures" (Code: OD07) — Maps internal human infrastructure setups, active cross-generational mentoring channels, and formalized expert-in-the-loop validation roles.

Task 6: Environmental Dependency Mapping (Category 8)
When expanding the Environmental Dependency category to evaluate macroscopic, physical, and infrastructure boundary conditions supporting or constraining knowledge pipelines, you must strictly map nodes into the following predefined sub-categories. The active sub-categories in this section must be strictly LESS THAN 6 in total. All sub-categories use the "ED" alphanumeric prefix.
* "Macro-Regulatory & Jurisdictional Frameworks" (Code: ED01) — Maps external national, regional, or international legal mandates, public policy safety guidelines, and safety statutory codes (e.g., statutory law, regional safety legislation frameworks) dictating baseline standard operating bounds.
* "Market Dynamics & Industry Baselines" (Code: ED02) — Maps industry macroeconomic traits, workforce structural supply conditions, demographic shifts, and foundational industry structural baselines (e.g., the one-item manufacturing model of construction sites).
* "Physical Site Conditions & Spatial Configuration" (Code: ED03) — Maps localized real-world geometry, layout dimensions, environmental boundaries, terrain hazards, structural positioning, and the physical constraints of active workspaces.
* "Socio-Technical Utilities & Infrastructure Access" (Code: ED04) — Maps the availability and stability of localized utility environments, regional data communication networks, wireless connectivity loops, or peripheral hardware installation access in rural or undeveloped construction contexts.

Task 7: Limitation Mapping (Category 9)
When expanding the Limitation category, you must strictly group nodes into predefined sub-categories. If the predefined sub-categories cannot match the nodes, you can expand the set of sub-categories, but strictly LESS THAN 10 active sub-categories in total.
1. "Human, Cognitive & Knowledge Retention Limitations" (Code: LI01) — Maps limits regarding Ebbinghaus's memory decay curves, lack of user motivation/engagement, training sample selection biases, and corporate amnesia via staff turnover.
2. "Technical, System & Data Limitations" (Code: LI02) — Maps system bottlenecks, technical latencies, lack of haptic feedback hardware, data format fragmentation, software extraction constraints (non-OCR), and statistically insignificant experimental outcomes.
3. "Cost, Resource & Structural Limitations" (Code: LI03) — Maps high financial costs, technology deployment resource limits, rigid uncustomized structural frameworks, and the complete absence of organizational continuous improvement loops.

